ISSUE #298 [closed] UDP-over-TCP session destructs immediately after handshake on macOS (TCP works fine)
created=2026-04-07T16:14:06Z updated=2026-04-19T13:22:57Z
## Environment

- **Client:** hev-socks5-tunnel `2.14.4 4d6c334` (official release binary)
- **OS:** macOS (Mac mini, Apple Silicon)
- **Server:** hev-socks5-server (latest release), IPv6-only
- **Server address:** Yggdrasil mesh IPv6 (`200::/7`)
- **Reference working client:** sockstun on Android with the same server and `udp: 'tcp'` — UDP works perfectly.

## Problem

UDP traffic does not pass through the tunnel on macOS. TCP works flawlessly through the same tunnel/server. The UoT session is established successfully (full SOCKS5 handshake completes, server returns OK response), but the session is destructed immediately after handshake without forwarding any UDP data.

## Minimal reproducer

`/tmp/test.yaml`:
```yaml
tunnel:
  name: utun9
  mtu: 1500
  ipv4: 198.19.0.1
  ipv6: 'fc00::2'
socks5:
  port: 1080
  address: <ipv6-server-address>
  udp: 'tcp'
  username: '...'
  password: '...'
misc:
  log-level: debug
  log-file: stderr
```

```bash
sudo hev-socks5-tunnel /tmp/test.yaml
# in another terminal:
sudo route add -net 8.8.8.8/32 -interface utun9
dig @8.8.8.8 example.com
```

## Observed behavior

`dig` times out. `tcpdump -i utun9 -n udp` shows UDP packets reaching the tunnel interface:
```
19:10:07.546797 IP 198.19.0.1.53291 > 8.8.8.8.53: 43612+ [1au] A? example.com. (40)
19:10:12.548654 IP 198.19.0.1.53291 > 8.8.8.8.53: 43612+ [1au] A? example.com. (40)
19:10:17.558754 IP 198.19.0.1.53291 > 8.8.8.8.53: 43612+ [1au] A? example.com. (40)
```

Debug log shows the UoT session is created, full SOCKS5 handshake completes successfully, then the session is destructed:
```
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 construct
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client construct
[I] 0x100e33030 socks5 client udp construct
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 session udp construct
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 session udp new
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 session run
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client connect [<server>]:1080
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 session udp bind
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client connect server fd 9
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client set auth
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client auth <user>:<pass>
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client handshake standard
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client write auth methods
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client read auth method
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client write auth creds
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client read auth creds
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client auth done
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client write request
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client read response
[I] 0x100e33030 socks5 client read response
[I] 0x100e33030 socks5 session handshake
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 session udp destruct      ← immediately after handshake
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client udp destruct
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 client destruct
[D] 0x100e33030 socks5 destruct
```

This pattern repeats for every retry from `dig`. TCP sessions to the same server through the same tunnel work perfectly (`socks5 session tcp splice` keeps the session alive).

## Cross-platform check

The exact same server with the same `udp: 'tcp'` mode works perfectly on **Android via sockstun**. Both clients use hev-socks5-tunnel core, but Android works and macOS does not.

## Additional context

- `hev-socks5-tunnel --version` → `2.14.4 4d6c334`
- TCP through the same tunnel: works (verified with `curl ifconfig.me` returning the proxy IP).
- `dig +tcp @8.8.8.8 example.com` through the tunnel: works.
- Tested with MTU 1500 and MTU 8500 — same behavior.
- Tested with `pipeline: true` — same behavior.
- Server reachable, server side IPv6, server runs latest hev-socks5-server.

Happy to provide pcap, full debug logs, or run additional diagnostics.
